Results
Cross-domain reasoning 1
Beyond spatial reasoning, multi-task training conferred cross-domain capabilities that no prior 2 cell-language model has demonstrated. The first is cross-species cell-type annotation. A model 3 trained on human cells should ideally transfer to mouse, and vice versa, but scGPT, Geneformer, 4 and CellPLM are each trained on a single species and cannot directly perform this task. We 5 probed cross-species reasoning with the cross-species cell-type task, in which OCellus is given a 6 mouse cell's top -ranked genes on the shared ortholog panel of approximately 250 genes and 7 asked to predict the canonical human-equivalent cell type. On a 2,000-cell subsample (1,000 8 mouse plus 1,000 human, 10 cell types), OCellus achieves 87.0 percent overall accuracy and at 9 least 95 percent on excitatory neurons, astrocytes, and neural-progenitor cells (Figure 7, 10 Supplementary Table S4). UMAP visualization on the gene-presence feature vector (Figure 7a; 11 300 mouse and 1,000 human cells shown for clarity) confirms that OCellus does not cluster 12 trivially by species: the two species interleave by cell type, indicating that the gene-presence 13 representation captures functional identity rather than species-specific sequence signatures. 14 Figure 7b colors the same UMAP by ground-truth cell type and Figure 7c colors it by OCellus 15 prediction, allowing cell-by-cell comparison of model performance. 16
